Creating a custom WordPress block with a heading, paragraph, and image involves several steps, including registering the block, defining its attributes, and rendering the block's content. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you achieve this:

### Step 1: Register the Block

First, you need to register your block with WordPress. This involves creating a PHP file in your theme's directory or a plugin, and then using the `register_block_type` function to register the block.

```php
// File: my-block.php

function register_my_block() {
    wp_register_script(
        'my-block-script',
        get_template_directory_uri() . '/js/my-block.js',
        array( 'wp-blocks', 'wp-element', 'wp-editor' )
    );

    wp_register_style(
        'my-block-styles',
        get_template_directory_uri() . '/css/my-block.css'
    );

    register_block_type( 'my-block' );
}
add_action( 'init', 'register_my_block' );
```

### Step 2: Define the Block Attributes

Next, you need to define the attributes for your block. This is done in the JavaScript file associated with your block. You'll define the attributes for the heading, paragraph, and image.

```javascript
// File: my-block.js

const { registerBlockType } = wp.blocks;
const { InspectorControls, RichText, MediaUpload, MediaUploadCheck } = wp.editor;

registerBlockType( 'my-block/my-block', {
    title: 'My Block',
    icon: 'format-image',
    category: 'common',
    attributes: {
        heading: {
            type: 'string',
            default: ''
        },
        paragraph: {
            type: 'string',
            default: ''
        },
        image: {
            type: 'object',
            default: null
        }
    },
    edit: Edit,
    save: Save,
} );

function Edit( { attributes, setAttributes, className } ) {
    const { heading, paragraph, image } = attributes;

    const handleImageChange = ( imageObject ) => {
        setAttributes( { image: imageObject } );
    };

    return (
        <div className={ className }>
            <InspectorControls>
                <MediaUploadCheck>
                    <MediaUpload
                        onSelect={ handleImageChange }
                        allowedTypes={ [ 'image' ] }
                        value={ attributes.image }
                        render={ ( { open } ) => (
                            <button className="button button-large" onClick={ open }>
